A selfish desire assumes attributes of a universal value, something that transcends personal experience. Thus it usurpes the place at the top of the motivation hierarchy and distorts the perception of the host it invaded. Such confusion of personal whims with the true universal order is characteristic for any manifestation of the inflated ego.
In this frame, the Demiurge represents the primal selfish psychic forces that constellate into ego. It is ignorant of the true divine source and assumes the role of God becoming a tyrant, hostile to anything that threatens to challenge the limits of its power, anything that transcends the illusions of grandeur.
The ego-demiurge also wrongly assumes the role of the master of its environment; and when it tries to dominate its circumstances, and inevitably fails, a bout of anger follows. Illusion of power -> impotence -> emotional disturbance. What leads to such inflation is alienation, separation from the source of true power; when you lose the connection you try to substitute the void with the phantoms, such as personal omniscience, that clearly don't belong in this place, they are usurpers.
As long as we live in the world created by ego, orchestrated by desires that assume the role of dharma, we are slaves stripped from the free will. To find the transcendental spark, the divine seed implanted in us by the true God, and to put it in its rightfull place at the top of the value hierarchy, is to liberate ourselves from this binding of false laws, escape the garden of delights, to become conscious and self-aware.
The path to freedom is the path of individuation, the exploration of spaces beyond the imposed collective laws and norms. The individual value hierarchy is achieved through making choices for one's self and accepting the personal responsibility for the outcomes. As the values fall into their right places, what happens is the unification of disjointed parts of our beings. To carry the cross is to carry the authorship of your choices and the burden of their outcomes. In this sense the new law of Jesus proclaims the individual freedom and the individual responsibility, in contrast to the old law of Moses that proclaims the collective norms that are designed to dominate the expansion of the Self. The individual that passed through the ordeals of individuation must then become the redeemer of the society that is governed by the rigid norms.

In the "Song of the Pearl" the salvation from the state of forgetting (unconsciousness, drunkness) comes in the form of a letter, i.e. a message. The message perceived truly, causes the seed to grow in us, and results in a spiritual transformation that connects the ego and the Self. Without this connection, this becoming a tree of life, we don't have an access to the psychic powers necessary to live out our personal mythologies (i.e. fulfillig our dreams). That's the only thing capable of giving you the capacity to liberate yourself from the reign of circumstances.
The pearl we must find and rescue is a thing of high value that is hidden behind the spume of days, the ouroboros, the karmic self-perpetuation of the manifested world. Our mission is to find those scattered sparks. An encounter with a thing of high value produces good memories which we send back to the spiritual world. So our mission is to collect good memories, find the soul of the world, we are the agent of divine expansion.
But we got distracted and enslaved by illusions during our mission, and we are in a need of a rescue ourselves.
After the awakening and rescuing of the transcendental souls, we are redeemed ourselves, which is another way to express the individuation, which is the goal of Yoga as well. In the song this arrival is symbolized by dressing in the regal robe that we left behind after leaving on our mission.

"The unconsciousness of human mind is the most terrifying of poverties, for to lack meaning is to lack the central core of the process of living.
"Self-knowledge establishes the axis that connect ego and self and leads to authentic living".


